# What a line ends with, decided here rather than by whoever last opened the file.
#
# This repository grew up across Windows and macOS and ended with 108 of its 1091 files on CRLF
# and the rest on LF, with no pattern to which was which. The cost is not tidiness: an editor
# that rewrites a file's endings turns a three-line change into a diff of the whole file, and a
# diff nobody can read is a change nobody reviews. That happened to middleware.ts, which is
# exactly the sort of file where it matters most.
#
# text=auto still lets Windows check files out with CRLF locally; eol=lf is what is stored.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is a Windows tool and its scripts are conventionally CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# A shell script with CRLF does not run: the interpreter on the shebang line comes back with a
# carriage return attached to its name.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Never rewritten, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.traineddata binary

# Packed extension: a binary, never diffed or rewritten.
*.zip binary
